Build Specification — App 34: The Cross-Subject Link Explorer
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste. Long lists are broken into short lines.

1. What It Is
A tool that takes a concept in one subject and shows how it appears in other subjects. The same concept can appear under different names in different fields, or under the same name with different meanings. The Cross-Subject Link Explorer records each concept with its subject of origin, the other subjects it appears in, the type of link between them, and the evidence. It is a map of how knowledge connects across the subjects we teach separately.

It reuses:

the STAG Schema Library (App 17)

the STAG Graph Viewer (App 5)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Method Tracker (App 32)

the Learning Provenance System (App 33)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for teachers, students, curriculum designers, and anyone who wants to see the connections between subjects.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of cross-subject links from users.

Store each link with the concept, the source subject, the target subject, the link type, and the provenance.

Record the concept name in each subject.

Record the link description: how the concept appears in the target subject.

Record the link type: same concept, analogous concept, prerequisite, special case, generalisation, or other.

Record the evidence: the sources that support the link.

Record the direction: the link may be symmetric or asymmetric.

Record the strength of the link: strong, moderate, weak.

Record the age range at which the link is typically taught.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by link ID, concept, source subject, target subject, link type, strength, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the concept description, the link description, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by source subject, by target subject, by link type, by strength, by year, by depositor.

Support exporting a link as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a link from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking a cross-subject link to a Method Tracker record if the concept is a method.

Support linking a cross-subject link to a Learning Provenance System record if the concept is a learned concept.

Support the cross-subject graph: a visualisation of the concepts as nodes and the links as edges, grouped by subject.

Support the subject map: a visualisation of the subjects as regions and the links as bridges between them.

Support the concept journey: a visualisation of how a single concept appears across subjects.

Support the link strength view: a visualisation of the strong and weak links between subjects.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical link in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 links.

Versioned: the explorer records the STAG schema version, the cross-subject link schema version, and the explorer version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

